      "writtenOn": "2014-04-13T15:14:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think that being able to generate your own tokens with whatever authorities you want is a bigger threat than being limited to whatever tokens currently exist.\n\nAlso, I don\u0027t see how having access to the CA secret key means that they necessarily have access to the token backend.\n\nSuggest remove this sentence.",
      "revId": "d93263d14f6c41b506f4520dfa7a5845b5ab9219",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAXH//dCY\u003d",
        "filename": "doc/source/configuration.rst",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 356,
      "author": {
        "id": 6488
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-04-21T16:42:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The sentence above does not imply that access to the CA secret key means that they have access to the token backend. It suggests that access to the keystone machine means direct access to the token backend.\n\nI\u0027ve clarified it to say that compromise of a Keystone service machine\u0027s filesystem security would likely mean direct access to the token backend.\n\nAnyway, it seems unlikely to me that one would set up a machine in such a way where an attacker would be able to read the CA secret key, but not /etc/keystone/keystone.conf.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAW3//iOk\u003d",
      "revId": "d93263d14f6c41b506f4520dfa7a5845b5ab9219",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
